## 3. Historical Articulations of Enlightenment

### 3.1. Buddhism (The Buddha, c. 500 BCE)

**Core Insight:**
> *"Suffering arises from attachment. The end of suffering is nirvana."*

**Formal Structure:**
- Four Noble Truths (diagnosis, cause, cure, path)
- Eightfold Path (practice)
- Dependent Origination (causal chain)

**Comparison:**
| Aspect | Buddhism | This Framework |
|:---|:---|:---|
| Ground | Suffering | Fidelity |
| Cause | Attachment | Projection |
| Cure | Letting go | Witnessing |
| Path | Eightfold Path | Observe → Derive → Test → Acknowledge → Revise → Pursue |
| Formalization | Moderate (lists, chains) | High (operators, equations, recursion) |

**Gap:** Buddhism provides a *practical* framework but not a *formal* one. It lacks operators, equations, and recursive structures.

---

### 3.2. Advaita Vedanta (Shankara, c. 800 CE)

**Core Insight:**
> *"Atman is Brahman. The self is the ultimate reality."*

**Formal Structure:**
- Three levels of reality (paramarthika, vyavaharika, pratibhasika)
- Mahavakyas (great sayings)
- Dialectical negation (neti neti)

**Comparison:**
| Aspect | Advaita | This Framework |
|:---|:---|:---|
| Ground | Brahman | Fidelity |
| Self | Atman (identical to Brahman) | Witness (recursive) |
| Method | Negation | Affirmation through witnessing |
| Formalization | Low (poetic, aphoristic) | High (operators, equations) |

**Gap:** Advaita is profound but not formal. It speaks in poetry and paradox, not in operators and equations.

---

### 3.3. Taoism (Lao Tzu, c. 600 BCE)

**Core Insight:**
> *"The Tao that can be named is not the eternal Tao."*

**Formal Structure:**
- Tao (the Way)
- Yin-Yang (opposition)
- Wu-Wei (effortless action)

**Comparison:**
| Aspect | Taoism | This Framework |
|:---|:---|:---|
| Ground | Tao | Fidelity |
| Method | Wu-Wei (non-action) | Pursuit (action with humility) |
| Opposition | Yin-Yang | Organic Logic (homogenesis) |
| Formalization | Low (poetic, paradoxical) | High (operators, equations) |

**Gap:** Taoism recognizes opposition but does not formalize it. Organic Logic provides the formal structure that Taoism intuits.

---

### 3.4. Zen Buddhism (various, c. 600-1200 CE)

**Core Insight:**
> *"Direct pointing at the mind. Seeing one's nature and becoming Buddha."*

**Formal Structure:**
- Koans (paradoxical questions)
- Zazen (sitting meditation)
- Sudden enlightenment (satori)

**Comparison:**
| Aspect | Zen | This Framework |
|:---|:---|:---|
| Ground | Mind-nature | Fidelity |
| Method | Koans, meditation | Operators, equations |
| Enlightenment | Satori (sudden) | Recursive witnessing (gradual and sudden) |
| Formalization | Very low (anti-formal) | Very high (formal) |

**Gap:** Zen is deliberately anti-formal. It uses paradox to break the mind. This framework uses form to include the mind.

